From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/3] Runtime PRIMEPOWER 250 CPU and interrupt support
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add runtime SPARC64 V recognition and PRIMEPOWER 250 support on top of
the M3000 architecture foundation. No V-specific Kconfig switch is needed.
The qualified PCI#01 pin-A source can route interrupts to either CPU;
other sources retain their firmware CPU0 route. This is not general
migration support for every bridge interrupt or shared pin.

The three platform patches cover CPU/MMU/SMP enablement, extended vector
normalization, and physical-map/U2U ownership with firmware and UART
handoff. Generic SPARC64, serial and network/storage changes are separate
groups. The M3000 Oberon/MSI follow-ups are not prerequisites.

The CPU patch validates supported MMU modes at startup and firmware
return, checks only actual kernel TLB reservations, and acknowledges
supported degradation before enabling interrupts. Huge pages and CPU
hotplug remain unsupported. Corrected incoming ECC needs an unimplemented
memory-repair step and is not falsely reported as recovered.

Only a qualified root-name query is admitted with the device active.
Orderly monitor/terminal entry requires explicit UART handoff and closed
device routes. Panic/oops/NMI refuse new firmware entry and require
external reset. Already-running firmware or CPU capture is not cancellable
by this code. Timer selectors remain unread; firmware routes are retained.

The exact cleaned UP and SMP images booted from disk to Gentoo and SSH.
Both passed native UART traffic, closed-device BREAK/go and active-device
BREAK refusal. Hash-checked PCI#01 transfers covered CPU0, CPU1, CPU1
close/reopen and return to CPU0, with interrupts counted only on the
selected CPU. Taint and bridge error counters remained zero.

Earlier instrumented images qualified the root-name firmware query and
CPU0/CPU1 RAM panic paths. Held-UART and simulated firmware-owned UART
panic tests recorded twelve attempted writes, zero UART MMIO and zero
CIF entry; external recovery completed. These fixtures do not cancel an
actual in-flight firmware call.

The same cleaned source also passed Ultra45 UP and eight-vCPU T5120
LDOM boot/reboot, fork/COW and hash-checked disk/network tests. Ultra45
passed native SUNSU serial traffic and BREAK/go. The LDOM uses eight
strands of one physical core and virtual I/O. Heavy serial-input bursts
during console output overran the Ultra45 UART on both old and new
kernels; paced input passed on both.

No ECC injection, broad shared-map migration, RTC UIE or arbitrary
active-device firmware coverage is claimed.

CPU references cite the SPARC64 V Implementation Supplement and JPS1.
The bridge protocol is based on captured firmware and hardware results;
no U2U register specification has been obtained. Codex assisted with code,
analysis and commit text during this development.

Magnus Lindholm:
  sparc64: add runtime SPARC64 V CPU, MMU and SMP support
  sparc64: normalize PRIMEPOWER 250 hardware interrupt vectors
  sparc64: manage PRIMEPOWER IRQ and firmware ownership

